artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a periodic two-phase composite occupying a bounded domain $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^3$, consisting of an elastic solid skeleton and a compressible viscous (Newtonian) fluid filling the pore space, with a small period $\varepsilon>0$. In the regime of small linearized vibrations (infinitesimal displacements, linearized Navier–Stokes for the fluid and linear elasticity for the solid, with linearized interface conditions on the moving solid–fluid boundary), it is classical that two-scale/periodic homogenization yields an effective macroscopic system of Biot–type poroelastic equations relating the macroscopic solid displacement, fluid pressure, and filtration velocity, with effective coefficients (drained elasticity tensor, Biot coupling, permeability via Darcy's law, etc.) determined by local cell problems.

Problem. Develop a rigorous homogenization theory for the same periodic solid–fluid mixture in the regime of general motion with large deformations. Specifically, starting from the coupled microscopic system

- nonlinear (finite-strain) elastodynamics for the solid phase, governed by a hyperelastic stored-energy density $W(F)$ with first Piola–Kirchhoff stress $P=\partial_F W$;
- compressible Navier–Stokes equations in the fluid phase, with constitutive law $p=p(ho)$ and viscous Cauchy stress depending on the symmetric velocity gradient;
- the genuine (nonlinearized) kinematic and dynamic interface conditions on the time-evolving solid–fluid interface, including continuity of velocity and traction in the deformed configuration;

posed in a domain with $\varepsilon$-periodic microstructure, derive the effective macroscopic equations governing the homogenized limit as $\varepsilon	o 0$. The task includes: (i) identifying the correct two-scale (Lagrangian and/or Eulerian) formulation suitable for finite deformations and a moving microgeometry; (ii) formulating and solving the corresponding nonlinear local cell problems; (iii) obtaining effective constitutive relations (analogues of the Biot stress–strain–pressure relation and of Darcy's law) valid for large deformations; and (iv) proving convergence of the microscopic solutions to solutions of the homogenized system in an appropriate functional setting.
